  "summary": "A style of mass housing developed during Leonid Brezhnev's rule, featuring larger apartments than Khrushchyovkas but still using prefabricated concrete panels.",
  "description": "These buildings typically had more amenities, better insulation, and sometimes decorative elements compared to earlier prefabricated housing. They reflected the Soviet Union's attempt to improve living standards while maintaining industrialized construction methods.",
  "culturalContext": "The brezhnevka — Leonid Brezhnev era housing (1964-1982) — succeeded the khrushchyovka with nine-to-sixteen story panels, elevators, garbage chutes, balconies, and modestly bigger rooms: the Soviet \"comfort generation\" block. Standard series (II-68, 121, P-44) built entire microrayons across the USSR; mocked as \"ship of the desert\" sameness, they are now nostalgic retro subjects (Instagram brezhnevka beauty pages) and still house vast populations — the era's ambiguous promise of socialist comfort made concrete.",
